## Fake "Free SpaceX Share" Claim Messages

Treat any message asking you to claim a free SpaceX share as a scam. When Elon Musk posted his pledge on X on October 19, 2026, he published no claim form, application website, or official sign-up process. Any email or text asking you to pay processing fees, send cryptocurrency, or provide your child's Social Security number to reserve stock is a phishing attempt.

If an equity gift ever reaches Trump Accounts, it will arrive automatically through federal donation rules rather than individual sign-up forms. Under temporary Treasury regulations [T.D. 10056](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2026/09/30/2026-20026/trump-accounts), corporate stock gifts must pass through an eligible 501(c)(3) charity as a general contribution divided equally across an entire qualified group. Families never need to pay fees, verify eligibility through third parties, or take action to reserve shares, as explained in our guide to [Elon Musk's SpaceX share for kids](/guides/elon-musk-spacex-share-for-kids/).

Official updates rely on strict federal channels. Legitimate notices arrive exclusively from no-reply@TrumpAccounts.Treasury.gov and the official portal at [TrumpAccounts.gov](https://trumpaccounts.gov), as confirmed by Treasury press release [SB-0642](https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0642). If an unsolicited message asks for your account password, demands payment, or directs you to an outside website, report it to the official Treasury call center at 1-866-USA-4547.